WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.939
CI/CD compromise can turn the software delivery pipeline into an attack path.

2
00:00:04.939 --> 00:00:12.562
Source control permissions, build runners, deployment tokens, artifact repositories, and release workflows need protection.

3
00:00:12.562 --> 00:00:19.516
Unsigned code and unverified artifacts make it difficult to prove that deployed software is authentic and unchanged.

4
00:00:19.516 --> 00:00:23.847
Integrity validation should cover the path from source to build to release.

5
00:00:23.847 --> 00:00:31.171
Insecure deserialization occurs when applications trust serialized input to reconstruct objects or behavior.

6
00:00:31.171 --> 00:00:38.714
Attackers may manipulate serialized data to alter state, bypass controls, or trigger unsafe server behavior.

7
00:00:38.714 --> 00:00:46.835
Build pipeline attacks are especially important for SaaS providers because a compromised pipeline can affect many customers at once.

8
00:00:46.835 --> 00:00:51.356
Change management, approvals, and monitoring help keep delivery trustworthy.

9
00:00:51.356 --> 00:00:54.718
Use code signing and artifact integrity validation.

10
00:00:54.718 --> 00:00:59.797
Secure CI/CD with least privilege, approvals, and monitored changes.

11
00:00:59.797 --> 00:01:03.858
Restrict and review changes to production software and data flows.

12
00:01:03.858 --> 00:01:08.019
Selected SOC 2 mappings for this topic include CC7.

13
00:01:08.019 --> 00:01:10.603
3 - Integrity monitoring; CC8.

14
00:01:10.603 --> 00:01:12.569
1 - Change management controls.
